From 057bddd7ec3b580f95bc7483ffff80d7ad9a0a81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 08:00:56 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Pickle top-level definitions shadowed by a package object
 member

The packageobjects phase enters the members of a package object,
declared or inherited, into the scope of the package class. Since
323e895672 (2009) `openPackageModule` first unlinks any existing entry
of the same name, so a top-level `object Foo` disappears from the
package scope when the package object has a member `Foo`.

The pickler and the backend find companions in that scope:

  - `PicklePhase` pickles a top-level class together with
    `companionSymbol`, which no longer finds the module, so the pickle of
    `class Foo` omits `object Foo`.
  - `genMirrorClass` asks for the pickle of
    `moduleClass.companionSymbol`, which is `NoSymbol`, so the mirror
    class gets only the `Scala` marker attribute that says the pickle is
    elsewhere. The pickle stored for the module is never written.

While the package object has the member, `Foo` resolves to it in this
run and in later ones alike, so nobody notices. Once the member is
removed and only the package object is recompiled, as an incremental
compiler does, the object can't be found on the classpath even though a
clean build compiles:

```
// A.scala
package a; trait PT { object Foo }; package object b extends a.PT
// B.scala
package a.b; object Foo

$ scalac -d out A.scala B.scala
$ javap -v out/a/b/Foo.class | grep -c ScalaSignature
0
// A.scala, edited
package a; trait PT; package object b extends a.PT
$ scalac -cp out -d out A.scala
$ scalac -cp out -d out Client.scala   // package a.b; object Client { Foo }
error: not found: value Foo
```

This commit stops depending on the package scope for these two lookups.
The pickler takes the companion from the compilation unit's top-level
definitions (top-level companions must be defined in the same file), and
the mirror class uses `moduleClass.sourceModule`. The unit-based lookup
also makes the `_.owner == sym.owner` filter that excluded package
object members from #6285 unnecessary.

Other scope-based companion lookups remain: for instance, a class whose
companion module is shadowed in this way still gets no static
forwarders. That only affects Java callers of a definition that Scala
code can't reach, so I've left it alone.

The test compiles the definitions, recompiles the package object without
the members, then compiles a client against the output. It covers a
declared and an inherited member, a `def` member, and a companion class.
Before this change the last step reports `not found: value` for each of
them.
